ts are removed, so any children that would be retained are not possible. So the condition \"node with retained children\" automatically holds if we only remove when the node's children are also being removed. So we must ensure that we are not trying to remove a node while some of its descendants have already been decided to be retained. Since we process deeper first, when we consider a leaf node, it has no children, so no retained children. When we consider an internal node later, we might have already removed some of its children? But if we remove a child, that child is gone. So when we later consider the parent, its children set is those not yet removed. If the parent has any child that is not removed (i.e., retained), we cannot remove the parent because it would remove those retained children. So we must check: if the node has any descendant (child, grandchild, etc.) that is not in the removal set? Actually, we are building a removal set. We need to check if the node's subtree (including itself) has any node that we have already decided to keep (i.e., not removed). Since we are processing bottom-up, at the time we process a node, any descendant that is either non-removable or we chose not to remove (maybe because required symbol) will still be present. So we cannot remove the parent. So we need to track which nodes have been removed. So we can have a set `removed_ids`. For a node to be removed, we require that all nodes in its subtree (including itself) are either already in removed_ids or are being considered for removal now, and none are retained. But we can't remove a node if any of its descendants are retained (not in removed_ids). So we need to check that for all descendants, they are either removable and we will remove them as part of this subtree removal, or they are already removed. Since we process bottom-up, when we consider a node, we have already decided on all its descendants (they were processed earlier). So we can simply check: if any descendant is