hem as candidates.\n\nAlso, the root's symbol is root, which is required. So root is never a candidate.\n\nNow, test with the first fixture case:\n\nNodes: root (id root, symbol root, depth0), n1 (s1, depth1, removable false), n2 (s2, depth1, removable true), n3 (s0, depth1, removable false), n4 (s1, depth2, removable true), n5 (s2, depth2, removable false), n6 (s0, depth2, removable true).\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1.\n\nCandidates: nodes with removable true and symbol not in required_set: s2 and s0? s0 is not required, s2 not required. So candidates: n2 (s2), n4? n4 has symbol s1 which is required, so not candidate. n6 (s0). Also check n1? n1 removable false. So candidates sorted by depth desc: depth2: n4? not candidate. n5 not candidate. n6 depth2 id n6. depth1: n2 depth1. n3 not removable. So sorted: (depth2, 'n6') then (depth1, 'n2').\n\nProcess n6: descendant set = {n6}. retained children: none (since no children). Subtree counts: s0:1. Total counts: root(s0? root symbol root), n1 s1, n2 s2, n3 s0, n4 s1, n5 s2, n6 s0. So total: root:1, s1:2, s2:2, s0:2. After removing n6, s0 goes to 1, still >0. Required symbols: root (1), s1 (2) both remain. So remove n6. removal_order = ['n6']. removed_ids = {'n6'}. Update total: s0:1.\n\nProcess n2: descendant set = {n2, n4}. n2 children: n4. Check retained children: descendant set = {n2, n4}. Is n4 in removed_ids? No, n4 not removed yet. So there is a child (n4) that is retained. Thus \"node with retained children\" -> cannot remove n2. So skip.\n\nThus only n6 removed. Retained IDs: all except n6: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']. Sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']. Return that.\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nNow, for the second fixture: it's similar. So output consistent.\n\nWe must ensure that we handle cases where multiple nodes can be removed, and removal of one might affect others. The bottom-up ordering and the retained children check should handle it.\n\nPotential pitfalls: When we remove a node, its descendants are added to removed_ids. Later, when we process an ancestor, we check retained children: if all descendants are removed, then it's fine to remove the ancestor. So that is correct.\n\nNow, write Python source code.\n\nWe'll define `solve(case)` function. Use `case` as dict. It has key \"payload\". So we need to access case['payload']['nodes'] etc.\n\nWe'll import collections.Counter.\n\nImplementation in Python:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\n    \n    # build maps\n    node_by_id = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\n        children[nid] = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    \n    # precompute descendant sets and subtree symbol counts via post-order\n    descendant_set = {}\n    subtree_counts = {}\n    # process nodes in reverse depth order (deepest first)\n    depth_groups = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        d = n['depth']\n        depth_groups.setdefault(d, []).append(n['id'])\n    depths = sorted(depth_groups.keys(), reverse=True)\n    for d in depths:\n        for nid in depth_groups[d]:\n            node = node_by_id[nid]\n            desc_set = {nid}\n            counts = Counter()\n            counts[node['symbol']] += 1\n            for cid in children[nid]:\n                desc_set.update(descendant_set[cid])\n                counts.update(subtree_counts[cid])\n            descendant_set[nid] = desc_set\n            subtree_counts[nid] = counts\n    \n    # initial total counts\n    total_counts = Counter()\n    for n in nodes:\n        total_counts[n['symbol']] += 1\n    \n    # candidates: removable, symbol not required\n    candidates = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['removable'] and n['symbol'] not in required_set and n['id'] != 'root':\n            candidates.append(n)\n    # sort by depth descending, then id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\n    \n    removed_ids = set()\n    removal_order = []\n    \n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid in removed_ids:\n            continue\n        # check retained children: any descendant other than self not removed\n        has_retained = False\n        for desc_id in descendant_set[nid]:\n            if desc_id != nid and desc_id not in removed_ids:\n                has_retained = True\n                break\n        if has_retained:\n            continue\n        # check required symbols still present after removal\n        subtree_counts_n = subtree_counts[nid]\n        possible = True\n        for sym in required_set:\n            if total_counts[sym] - subtree_counts_n.get(sym, 0) <= 0:\n                possible = False\n                break\n        if not possible:\n            continue\n        # perform removal\n        for desc_id in descendant_set[nid]:\n            removed_ids.add(desc_id)\n            # update total counts (we do it after adding all descendants)\n        # subtract subtree counts\n        total_counts.subtract(subtree_counts_n)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    # retained = nodes not removed\n    retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_ids]\n    retained_ids.sort()\n    return {\"retained\": retained_ids, \"removed\": removal_order}\n```\n\nBut note: we added all descendant IDs to removed_ids. However, we already added some descendants that might have been processed earlier (like if a child was removed individually, it would already be in removed_ids). But we are adding them again, which is fine as set. Also, we subtract total_counts once for the whole subtree. That's correct.\n\nWe also need to be careful: when we process descendants, we might have already removed some of them? But we already checked that there are no retained children, meaning all descendants are not removed yet? Actually, the condition \"has_retained\" checks if any descendant is not removed. So if there is any descendant not removed, we skip. So all descendants are either already removed (if we removed them as separate earlier) or not removed. Since we haven't removed any descendant as separate because we process deeper first—but we might have removed a descendant if it was a candidate itself and we removed it earlier. For example, consider a leaf node that is removable and we removed it. Then later when we consider its parent, the leaf is already in removed_ids. So the parent's descendant set includes that leaf, but it is removed, so has_retained returns False because all descendants are either the node itself (nid) and others are removed. So condition passes. Then we proceed to remove the parent. That is correct: we can remove an internal node after all its children have been removed.\n\nOne issue: when we remove a node, we add all its descendants (including those already removed) to removed_ids again (set unaffected). Then we subtract subtree_counts, which includes counts for those already removed. But total_counts has already been subtracted for those removed descendants (when they were removed individually). So we would be subtracting counts twice? That would be wrong. We need to avoid double subtraction. Therefore, we should not subtract the counts for descendants that have already been removed. The simplest fix: when we remove a node, we should only subtract the counts for nodes that are not already in removed_ids. But we also need to add them to removed_ids. Since we already verified that all descendants are either removed (already in removed_ids) or not removed? Wait, we checked that there are no retained children (i.e., all descendants except self are in removed_ids). That means ALL descendants are already in removed_ids. Because if any descendant was not removed, that